I'd recommend sticking with map(). The reason is that you're already using recurse(), which implies you're doing some sort of top-down recursion; the convention in Go for top-down recursions is to use a loop instead of map(). Of course, ultimately the choice is yours. But I'd want to review this code before it gets checked in. That's because it'll be easy to get the order wrong and map() will silently compile to a struct literal instead of a loop. I'd want to test it thoroughly to make sure it's working as desired. (And yes, I've been bitten by this before.) I'll write up a test here so I can easily review them later. (Also, if we're going to use map(), I'd recommend doing it in the case statement instead of in the recurse() call. That'll avoid the possibility of the map() being miscompiled.) 

```go
func fn(input []int) int {
    return recurse(input)
}

func recurse(input []int) int {
    if len(input) == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    cases := []struct {
        cond    func() bool
        result  int
    }{
        {fn(input[1:]), input[0]},
        {false, recurse(input[1:])},
    }
    for _, c := range cases {
        if c.cond() {
            return c.result
        }
    }
    panic("unreachable")
}
```
